🧭 The Big Picture Flow

When you run npm run backend (or npm run dev), this exact chain reaction happens inside backend/:

Let's examine each file, what it does, and why it was built this way.

File 1: backend/server.js — The Engine Room

This is the main entry point of the entire backend API.

1. Why dotenv.config() is at the very top (Line 7):
javascript
dotenv.config();
Node.js does not natively understand .env files.
dotenv.config() must run first so environment variables (MONGO_URI, JWT_SECRET, PORT) are injected into Node's process.env before any other file tries to read them.
2. Why cors(...) is essential (Line 15–18):
javascript
app.use(cors({
    origin: process.env.CLIENT_URL || 'http://localhost:5173',
    credentials: true
}));
The Problem: Your React frontend runs on port 5173, while your Express backend runs on port 5000. By default, web browsers block requests between different ports for security (called CORS: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing).
The Solution: This middleware tells the browser: "Allow requests coming from http://localhost:5173 to safely talk to port 5000."
3. Why express.json() is needed (Line 19):
javascript
app.use(express.json());
When a user logs in, the browser sends { "email": "...", "password": "..." } as raw text.
express.json() automatically parses that incoming text and converts it into a ready-to-use JavaScript object inside req.body.
4. The Keep-Alive Health Route (Line 23–29):
javascript
app.get('/api/health', (req, res) => {
    res.status(200).json({ status: 'online', service: 'Fenaria Art Studio Backend' });
});
Why this is critical for Render: Render's free tier puts web services to sleep after 15 minutes of inactivity.
By setting up a free 10-minute ping (e.g., using UptimeRobot
) to https://your-backend.onrender.com/api/health, the server stays awake 24/7 at zero cost!
File 2: backend/config/db.js — The Cloud Database Connector

This file manages the connection to your MongoDB Atlas cluster.

1. The DNS Fix for Windows / Indian ISPs (Line 4–9):
javascript
try {
    dns.setServers(['8.8.8.8', '8.8.4.4', '1.1.1.1']);
} catch (e) {}
The Problem: Many Indian ISPs (Jio, Airtel) and Windows DNS configurations fail to resolve MongoDB's mongodb+srv:// SRV records, throwing an ECONNREFUSED querySrv error.
The Solution: This forces Node.js to use Google's Public DNS (8.8.8.8), preventing connection drops.
2. Fast Fail Timeout (Line 14–16):
javascript
const conn = await mongoose.connect(uri, {
    serverSelectionTimeoutMS: 5000 // Fast fail in 5s instead of hanging for 30s
});
By default, if your IP is not whitelisted, Mongoose freezes your server for 30 seconds before failing.
We reduced this to 5 seconds and added a clear warning log (If using MongoDB Atlas, make sure your IP is whitelisted) so you know immediately if your IP changed.
File 3: backend/models/User.js — The Customer Blueprint

This file replaces the old MySQL user table with a modern MongoDB Schema.

1. Schema Validation (Line 4–59):
Defines fields (name, email, password, phone_number, address, city, state, zip_code, role, is_online).
Enforces data integrity: email must be unique and match a valid email format, password must be at least 6 characters.
2. Automatic Password Hashing via bcryptjs (Line 62–71):
javascript
userSchema.pre('save', async function (next) {
    if (!this.isModified('password')) return next();
    const salt = await bcrypt.genSalt(10);
    this.password = await bcrypt.hash(this.password, salt);
    next();
});
Pre-save hook: Runs automatically right before a user document is saved to MongoDB.
Takes the raw password (e.g., "secret123"), mixes it with a random 10-round cryptographic salt, and turns it into an irreversible hash (e.g., "$2a$10$wT8K...").
Raw passwords are NEVER stored in the database.
3. Secure Verification & JSON Sanitation (Line 74–83):
javascript
userSchema.methods.comparePassword = async function (candidatePassword) {
    return bcrypt.compare(candidatePassword, this.password);
};
userSchema.methods.toJSON = function () {
    const userObject = this.toObject();
    delete userObject.password;
    return userObject;
};
comparePassword: Safely compares entered text against the hash during login.
toJSON: Automatically removes the password field whenever user data is sent back to the frontend. The browser never receives password hashes!
Summary Checklist for Point 2:
server.js starts the app, applies CORS, and mounts routes.
config/db.js securely links to MongoDB Atlas with DNS & timeout protections.
models/User.js enforces customer structure and encrypts passwords automatically.

